[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SOLID Principles in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"SOLID is an acronym representing five fundamental principles of object-oriented programming and design. These principles, when applied correctly, help create maintainable, flexible, and robust software. This tutorial will explore each principle with C# examples."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A class should have only one reason to change. In other words, a class should have only one responsibility. This principle promotes high cohesion and low coupling."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Violation of SRP
public class User
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public void SaveToDatabase()
    {
        // Database logic here
    }

    public void SendEmail(string message)
    {
        // Email sending logic here
    }
}

// Correct implementation
public class User
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

public class UserRepository
{
    public void Save(User user)
    {
        // Database logic here
    }
}

public class EmailService
{
    public void SendEmail(User user, string message)
    {
        // Email sending logic here
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the first example, the 'User' class has two responsibilities: managing user data and handling database operations and sending emails. The corrected implementation separates these responsibilities into 'UserRepository' and 'EmailService' classes, adhering to SRP."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Software entities (classes, modules, functions, etc.) should be open for extension, but closed for modification. This means you should be able to add new functionality without altering existing code."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Violation of OCP
public class Rectangle
{
    public double Width { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }
}

public class Circle
{
    public double Radius { get; set; }
}

public class AreaCalculator
{
    public double CalculateArea(object shape)
    {
        if (shape is Rectangle)
        {
            Rectangle rect = (Rectangle)shape;
            return rect.Width * rect.Height;
        }
        else if (shape is Circle)
        {
            Circle circle = (Circle)shape;
            return Math.PI * circle.Radius * circle.Radius;
        }

        throw new ArgumentException("Invalid shape type");
    }
}

// Correct implementation
public interface IShape
{
    double CalculateArea();
}

public class Rectangle : IShape
{
    public double Width { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }

    public double CalculateArea()
    {
        return Width * Height;
    }
}

public class Circle : IShape
{
    public double Radius { get; set; }

    public double CalculateArea()
    {
        return Math.PI * Radius * Radius;
    }
}

public class AreaCalculator
{
    public double CalculateArea(IShape shape)
    {
        return shape.CalculateArea();
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The first example violates OCP because adding a new shape requires modifying the 'AreaCalculator' class. The corrected implementation uses an 'IShape' interface, allowing new shapes to be added without modifying 'AreaCalculator'."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without altering the correctness of the program. In simpler terms, if you have a base class and a derived class, you should be able to use the derived class wherever the base class is expected without causing unexpected behavior."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Violation of LSP
public class Rectangle
{
    public virtual double Width { get; set; }
    public virtual double Height { get; set; }
}

public class Square : Rectangle
{
    public override double Width
    {
        get { return base.Width; }
        set { base.Width = value; base.Height = value; }
    }

    public override double Height
    {
        get { return base.Height; }
        set { base.Width = value; base.Height = value; }
    }
}

public class AreaCalculator
{
    public double CalculateArea(Rectangle rect)
    {
        rect.Width = 5;
        rect.Height = 10;

        return rect.Width * rect.Height; // Unexpected result for Square
    }
}

//Correct Implementation
public interface IReadOnlyRectangle
{
    double Width { get; }
    double Height { get; }
}

public class Rectangle : IReadOnlyRectangle
{
    public double Width { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }
}

public class Square : IReadOnlyRectangle
{
    public double Side { get; set; }
    public double Width { get { return Side; } }
    public double Height { get { return Side; } }
}

public class AreaCalculator
{
    public double CalculateArea(IReadOnlyRectangle rect)
    {
        return rect.Width * rect.Height;
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the first example, 'Square' inherits from 'Rectangle'. Setting the width of a 'Square' also changes its height, violating LSP because the behavior is different from a regular 'Rectangle'. The corrected implementation uses interfaces to define read-only properties, avoiding the issue."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. ISP suggests that instead of having one large interface, it's better to have multiple smaller, more specific interfaces."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Violation of ISP
public interface IWorker
{
    void Work();
    void Eat();
}

public class Worker : IWorker
{
    public void Work()
    {
        // Working logic
    }

    public void Eat()
    {
        // Eating logic
    }
}

public class Robot : IWorker
{
    public void Work()
    {
        // Working logic
    }

    public void Eat()
    {
        // Robots don't eat, but must implement this method
        throw new NotImplementedException();
    }
}

// Correct implementation
public interface IWorkable
{
    void Work();
}

public interface IEatable
{
    void Eat();
}

public class Worker : IWorkable, IEatable
{
    public void Work()
    {
        // Working logic
    }

    public void Eat()
    {
        // Eating logic
    }
}

public class Robot : IWorkable
{
    public void Work()
    {
        // Working logic
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the first example, the 'Robot' class is forced to implement the 'Eat' method, even though robots don't eat. The corrected implementation separates the 'IWorker' interface into 'IWorkable' and 'IEatable', allowing 'Robot' to only implement 'IWorkable'."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions. This principle promotes loose coupling and makes the system more flexible."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Violation of DIP
public class LightBulb
{
    public void TurnOn()
    {
        // Turn on logic
    }

    public void TurnOff()
    {
        // Turn off logic
    }
}

public class Switch
{
    private LightBulb bulb;

    public Switch()
    {
        bulb = new LightBulb();
    }

    public void Operate()
    {
        bulb.TurnOn();
    }
}

// Correct implementation
public interface ISwitchable
{
    void TurnOn();
    void TurnOff();
}

public class LightBulb : ISwitchable
{
    public void TurnOn()
    {
        // Turn on logic
    }

    public void TurnOff()
    {
        // Turn off logic
    }
}

public class Switch
{
    private ISwitchable device;

    public Switch(ISwitchable device)
    {
        this.device = device;
    }

    public void Operate()
    {
        device.TurnOn();
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the first example, the 'Switch' class directly depends on the 'LightBulb' class. The corrected implementation introduces an 'ISwitchable' interface, allowing the 'Switch' class to depend on an abstraction rather than a concrete implementation. This makes it easier to switch to a different type of device (e.g., a fan) without modifying the 'Switch' class."},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"By applying these SOLID principles, you can create more maintainable, flexible, and robust C# applications."}]
